WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.382
This book is not moving, but
the foam beneath it is squashed.

2
00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:06.250
Is the foam doing nothing?

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.974
Watch the contact patch.

4
00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:11.566
The book presses down, and
the foam changes shape.

5
00:00:11.566 --> 00:00:14.512
What might the foam
be doing to the book?

6
00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:17.404
The book and foam
interact through contact.

7
00:00:17.404 --> 00:00:20.699
Book on foam pushes down;
foam on book pushes up.

8
00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:23.254
Those arrows act on
different objects.

9
00:00:23.254 --> 00:00:27.356
To explain the resting book,
follow forces on the book: Earth

10
00:00:27.356 --> 00:00:31.256
on book points down, while
foam on book support points up.

11
00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:35.200
The book remains still while
the foam is supporting it.

12
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.427
Remove the foam, and
the book begins to fall.

13
00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:42.012
Put the support back, and
the book can rest again.

14
00:00:42.012 --> 00:00:45.024
Stillness is not proof
that no forces act.

15
00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:54.995
Add a second identical book.

16
00:00:54.995 --> 00:00:56.621
The foam squashes further.

17
00:00:56.621 --> 00:01:00.059
To support the resting stack,
the foam's upward push is

18
00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.121
greater than it
was for one book.

19
00:01:02.121 --> 00:01:05.747
A longer arrow shows greater
force within this comparison.

20
00:01:05.747 --> 00:01:07.324
Now move to the cart.

21
00:01:07.324 --> 00:01:11.379
A robot hand touches the
cart and pushes to the right.

22
00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:13.857
Hand on cart is
an applied force.

23
00:01:13.857 --> 00:01:18.288
The arrow begins on the cart
and points in the direction of

24
00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:18.964
the push.

25
00:01:18.964 --> 00:01:21.303
A rubber band can
pull the same cart.

26
00:01:21.303 --> 00:01:23.643
The band stretches
towards the robot.

27
00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:26.741
Rubber band on cart points
towards the robot too.

28
00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:29.965
Push and pull describe the
direction of the applied

29
00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:33.253
interaction; neither is
material stored in the cart.

30
00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:44.457
We can use band stretch
as a comparison meter.

31
00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:47.312
Mark the resting length
as zero centimetres.

32
00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:50.881
Then read how many centimetres
the band stretches while

33
00:01:50.881 --> 00:01:51.400
pulling.

34
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.033
More stretch means a greater
pull in this investigation.

35
00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:57.693
These are relative
readings, not Newtons.

36
00:01:57.693 --> 00:02:00.871
Keep the cart, surface,
and start line the same.

37
00:02:00.871 --> 00:02:04.314
First, hold a two-centimetre
stretch for one second.

38
00:02:04.314 --> 00:02:04.712
Reset.

39
00:02:04.712 --> 00:02:08.089
Then hold a four-centimetre
stretch for one second.

40
00:02:08.089 --> 00:02:12.194
Change only the stretch reading
and measure the distance after

41
00:02:12.194 --> 00:02:13.121
the same time.

42
00:02:13.121 --> 00:02:16.571
At two centimetres, the cart
travels eighty centimetres.

43
00:02:16.571 --> 00:02:19.836
At four centimetres, it
travels one hundred and fifty

44
00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:20.575
centimetres.

45
00:02:20.575 --> 00:02:24.087
The larger stretch reading
goes with the larger change in

46
00:02:24.087 --> 00:02:25.319
motion in this test.

47
00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:28.153
The data supports a
stronger-pull explanation.

48
00:02:36.374 --> 00:02:40.532
Now test the claim: a table
does nothing because it does not

49
00:02:40.532 --> 00:02:40.878
move.

50
00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:43.442
Place the foam and
book on the table.

51
00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:47.738
The contact patch changes shape,
and the book rests instead of

52
00:02:47.738 --> 00:02:48.847
passing through.

53
00:02:48.847 --> 00:02:51.549
The surface is part
of the interaction.

54
00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:55.627
Which model fits the forces
acting on a book resting on foam?

55
00:02:55.627 --> 00:02:58.167
One downward
Earth-on-book arrow only?

56
00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.173
No arrows because it is still?

57
00:03:00.173 --> 00:03:04.050
Or Earth on book down paired
with foam on book support up?

58
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.724
Choose, then check
the contact evidence.

59
00:03:06.724 --> 00:03:07.780
Use this routine.

60
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:08.899
Name both objects.

61
00:03:08.899 --> 00:03:09.955
Mark the contact.

62
00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:12.379
Draw the force on
the receiving object.

63
00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:14.802
Point the arrow in
the force direction.

64
00:03:14.802 --> 00:03:18.344
When comparing pulls, use the
same meter and a fair test.

65
00:03:18.344 --> 00:03:20.208
Even a still surface can push.

66
00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:23.750
Evidence makes that invisible
interaction understandable.
